{
  "_provenance": {
    "what": "The junction stars (yogatārā) of Sūrya-Siddhānta ch.8 as the text gives them: a dhruvaka (polar longitude) and a vikṣepa (polar latitude) for each, plus the named stars of 8.10-8.12. Both coordinates hang from the dhruva: the dhruvaka is the point of the ecliptic cut by the circle drawn from the dhruva through the star.",
    "source": "editions/surya-siddhanta-full-edition.html, verses 8.1-8.12 (lines 3723-3870). Numbers are the verses' own numeral words; the test re-derives every value from them.",
    "rule_8_1": "dhruvaka in arc-minutes = (bhoga index − 1) × 800 + figure × 10, the bhoga being one twenty-seventh of the circle. Uttarāṣāḍhā, Abhijit, Śravaṇa and Dhaniṣṭhā are placed by the positional rules of 8.4-8.5 instead of a figure.",
    "frame": "Polar coordinates belong to the epoch of the observation: when the dhruva moves among the stars over centuries, they change. The test converts them to ecliptic places, which carry across epochs. The longitudes are sidereal from the text's own Meṣa 0; turning them into right ascensions needs the ayanāṃśa of the moment, which the owner measures.",
    "flags": "Uttarāṣāḍhā's vikṣepa (5° south, the word 'iṣavaḥ' in 8.8) is this repository's reading; the edition's English rendering of 8.8 omits it. Prajāpati (8.20) is placed both 'five degrees east of Brahmahṛdaya' and 'at the end of Vṛṣa', which disagree by 3°; it is kept with that flag and not used."
  },
